<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russo-Turkish War (1806–1812)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russo-Turkish War of 1806–1812 was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, mainly in the Danubian principalities. After years of indecisive campaigning, Mikhail Kutuzov trapped the main Ottoman army near Ruse in 1811. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of May 1812, signed just before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> to Russia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812.jpg" alt="Russo-Turkish War (1806–1812)" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexey Bogolyubov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athosbattle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war broke out during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. In 1806 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a>, encouraged by the Russian defeat at Austerlitz and advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, deposed the pro-Russian Constantine Ypsilantis as Hospodar of Wallachia and Alexander Mourousis as Hospodar of Moldavia. France had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and threatened to enter the Danubian Principalities, so a Russian contingent of 40,000 advanced into Moldavia and Wallachia to protect the border. The Sultan blocked the Dardanelles to Russian ships and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> was reluctant to concentrate large forces against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> while his army was fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. A large Ottoman offensive towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> was checked at Obilesti on 2 June 1807 by about 4,500 men under Mikhail Miloradovich, and Ivan Gudovich beat a force of 20,000 at Arpachai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Dmitry Senyavin's navy blockaded the Dardanelles and defeated the Ottoman fleet, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a> was deposed, and the fleet was destroyed the next month at the Battle of Athos. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Peace of Tilsit</a> then brought an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> used to bring more soldiers south, raising its army to 80,000.</p><p>Under Pyotr Bagration and then the brothers Nikolay and Sergei Kamensky the Russians took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silistra">Silistra</a> and Rousse in 1810, but they had won no victory decisive enough to end the war.</p><p>With relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> worsening, Alexander appointed Mikhail Kutuzov, whom he disliked but needed, to command. Kutuzov beat a larger army under Grand Vizier Ahmed Pasha at Rusçuk on 22 June 1811, then withdrew across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. When 36,000 Turkish troops crossed and set up a fortified bridgehead near Slobozia, the Russians surrounded them. On the night of 1 October a Russian detachment of 7,500 crossed the river and routed the camp on the other bank. The besieged Turks suffered from bombardment, malnutrition and disease, and Ahmed Pasha surrendered on 14 November 1811.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> was signed on 28 May 1812, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> approved it on 11 June, 13 days before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion began. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ceded the eastern half of Moldavia, the territory east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and Russia became a new power on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In Transcaucasia Turkey regained Poti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapa">Anapa</a> and Akhalkalaki, while Russia kept Sukhum-Kale and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> accepted its annexation of Imereti in 1810. Some sources describe the war as a Russian victory, and others call it indecisive because Russia returned all occupied land except Bessarabia. The commanders brought many soldiers back to the west before Napoleon's attack.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russo-Turkish War (1806–1812)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Russo-Turkish War</b> of 1828, the kish otaman Osyp Hladky defected to Russia with part of the Host.</blockquote><a href="/state/danube-sich">Danube Sich</a></li></ul></section>
